=== RELATED-WORK / POSITIONING TEXT UNDER REVIEW ===
# Related Work & Positioning — RankCRC

> Current positioning for the RankCRC headline (replaces the stale CU-GR `related_positioning.md`, kept for history). Locked / experiment-independent. The contribution is **distribution-free risk-controlled selective reliability** for recommendation, not raw-ranking SOTA.

## Conformal prediction and conformal risk control
Split conformal prediction \citep{vovk2005algorithmic,angelopoulos2023gentle} gives distribution-free coverage for set-valued predictors; Conformal Risk Control \citep{angelopoulos2024crc} extends this to any bounded, monotone loss, yielding `E[L(λ̂)] ≤ α` in marginal expectation. RCPS \citep{bates2021rcps} and Learn-then-Test \citep{angelopoulos2021ltt} give the high-probability (PAC) analogue via multiple-testing. **RankCRC's difference:** the controlled object is **listwise rank-risk** (`1 − NDCG@k`) on the **served slice after abstention** (a self-normalized conditional mean), not a pointwise loss over the full population. We show plain CRC does not attach to the naive served-slice estimator (random-denominator winner's curse) and repair it with a frozen-`λ` B1/B2 split so the served mean is an honest i.i.d. mean; we name LTT as the PAC upgrade and a numerator+coverage-floor route as an exact surrogate. To our knowledge, no prior work provides a distribution-free marginal-expectation bound on **top-`k` listwise NDCG risk among accepted users using a label-free frozen selector** in recommendation (we state this narrowly, "to our knowledge," not as a broad first-of-kind).

## Conformal selective prediction / abstention WITH guarantees
Beyond heuristic abstention, a line of work gives *guaranteed* selective prediction: risk-controlling prediction sets \citep{bates2021rcps}, conformalized selective classification and abstention systems built on RCPS/Learn-then-Test, distribution-free reliability guarantees applied directly to recommendation \citep{angelopoulos2023recsys}, and two-stage conformal risk control for ranked retrieval \citep{xu2025twostage}. These control pointwise, set-coverage, or stage-wise retrieval losses, typically over the full population or via prediction-set size. **RankCRC's difference (vs.\ the two closest neighbors):** \citet{angelopoulos2023recsys} attach a distribution-free guarantee to the *size/coverage of a returned candidate set* over the *full* user population; \citet{xu2025twostage} control *stage-wise retrieval losses* (e.g.\ recall at a cutoff) for a two-stage pipeline via LTT/CRC, again marginally over all queries. RankCRC instead controls a *conditional* served-slice **listwise NDCG** risk `E[1−NDCG@k | served]` — a self-normalized conditional mean with a *random* served count among users an abstention policy chose to serve — with a *label-free* rank-geometry selector and the frozen-`λ` B1/B2 discipline that makes the conditional-mean certificate valid (neither neighbor controls a post-abstention conditional listwise mean, and neither uses a label-free served-slice selector). RankCRC is best read as *transferring* this guaranteed-abstention machinery to recommendation with a recsys-specific listwise target — a protocol/application contribution, not a new conformal theorem.

## Selective prediction / classification WITHOUT guarantees
SelectiveNet \citep{geifman2019selectivenet} and the risk–coverage literature learn an abstention head trading coverage for accuracy, but provide **no distribution-free finite-sample risk guarantee** — the risk–coverage curve is empirical. **RankCRC's difference:** it adds the CRC marginal-expectation bound on top of abstention, and the selector is *label-free* over rank-geometry features rather than a jointly-trained confidence head. We include a SelectiveNet-without-guarantee baseline (trained on Fold A only, matched coverage) precisely to isolate that the *guarantee* — not merely "abstain on hard users" — is the contribution.

## LLM-uncertainty calibration
Verbalized-confidence and calibration work (ECE, temperature scaling, reliability diagrams; Kadavath et al.; Tian et al.) asks whether an LLM's stated confidence matches accuracy. **RankCRC makes no ECE / reliability-diagram claim**: the object is an operational *served-slice risk bound*, a different operational guarantee than calibration of a confidence number (different in kind — a risk bound on a served sub-population — not a strictly stronger version of ECE). Our confidence `g` is not the LLM's verbalized confidence but a logistic model over rank-geometry of the base ranker's candidate scores, with the held-out label forbidden as a feature.

## Selective / reliable recommendation and LLM-for-RecSys
Prior LLM-for-recommendation work (the 8 official baselines: ELMRec, IRLLRec, LLM2Rec, LLMEmb, LLM-ESR, ProEx, ProMax, RLMRec) targets raw-ranking improvement on the full user population. Selective / cautious recommendation has been explored heuristically (confidence thresholds, popularity gating) without distribution-free guarantees. **RankCRC's difference:** it reframes the problem as *risk-controlled reliability* — serve the slice on which the served-slice rank-risk `E[1−NDCG@k | served]` is bounded by `α` in marginal expectation — orthogonal to raw SOTA, and demonstrates (the empirical contribution, GPU-gated) that a deployable label-free-at-inference selector holds the bound across 8 domains with non-trivial coverage and transfers across domains/rankers. The sibling pointwise-posterior method (pony) owns the raw-ranking lane; TRUCE owns the reliability lane.

## Why this is not "CRC applied to NDCG"
The strongest reviewer objection is incrementality. The novelty is the *combination*: (i) listwise rank-risk as the controlled quantity; (ii) the served-slice selective framing; (iii) a **label-free, deployable** confidence over rank-geometry (no test peek), repaired with the frozen-`λ` split so the conditional-mean certificate is valid; (iv) a *falsifiable* gain law that predicts the served-slice lift a priori; and (v) the 8-domain feasibility triad (cross-domain risk control + non-trivial coverage at useful `α` + label-free confidence transfer) — which no prior conformal-recsys work \citep{angelopoulos2023recsys,xu2025twostage} demonstrates. The value is orthogonal to raw SOTA: a "serve only when the served-slice rank-risk is bounded in marginal expectation" policy is operationally useful even when raw ranking is mid-pack.

> **Citation status (citation-audit, 2026-06-20):** RESOLVED for the conformal core. The by-category prose above is now keyed to concrete, bibliographically-verified references in `refs/conformal.bib` (Vovk–Gammerman–Shafer 2005; Angelopoulos & Bates gentle intro; Conformal Risk Control, ICLR 2024; RCPS, JACM 2021; Learn-then-Test 2021; Angelopoulos et al. distribution-free recsys reliability, COPA 2023; Xu et al. two-stage risk control for ranked retrieval, IJCAI 2025; SelectiveNet, ICML 2019). Each entry's authors/year/venue were verified before inclusion — none fabricated. **Still pending (needs the experiment phase):** baseline/method citations for the 8 official LLM-for-RecSys systems and any RankCRC empirical-claim citations are added once the results block exists; verify each new entry the same way before adding.

