codex
The defense is **partly convincing**, but too dependent on a narrow phrasing. I would not collapse RankCRC to Angelopoulos et al. COPA 2023: that work is about returned recommendation sets with FDR-style reliability over the full population, not conditional served-user listwise risk. The Xu et al. IJCAI 2025 comparison is weaker: Xu explicitly handles ranked retrieval and nDCG-like ranking risk, so “listwise NDCG” alone is not enough. The real distinction must be the **post-abstention conditional target with random served count plus frozen B1/B2 threshold discipline**.

The strongest reviewer objection: “This is just CRC/LTT or holdout risk estimation applied after a selective filter; label-free scores at inference are standard.” The text partially rebuts this by admitting it is a protocol/application contribution, not a new conformal theorem. But it should more directly say why Xu-style all-query/stagewise risk does **not** certify `E[loss | served]` when the served set is data-selected.

Citation issues: Angelopoulos 2023 is slightly misdescribed as “size/coverage”; it is more accurately FDR / “mostly good returned items” with diversity optimization. Xu 2025 is under-described as recall-stage control; it includes ranking-risk formulations inspired by nDCG. More importantly, the positioning is missing obvious neighbors: De Toni et al., **“You Don’t Bring Me Flowers”**, RecSys 2025 CRC for unwanted recommendations; Fair Learning to Rank with Distribution-Free Risk Control; conformal IR / retrieval-set work such as score-refinement for conformal information retrieval; and newer selective conformal risk-control / reject-option work. These do not kill the claim, but omitting them makes the novelty audit look cherry-picked.

The “to our knowledge” claim is appropriately hedged only if the exact conjunction is preserved: **top-k listwise NDCG risk, among accepted users, marginal-expectation bound, label-free frozen selector, recommendation setting**. It is over-engineered and fragile if used as the main novelty sentence. Make the novelty conceptual, not a qualifier stack.

Sources checked: [Angelopoulos et al. 2023](https://proceedings.mlr.press/v204/angelopoulos23a.html), [Xu et al. 2025](https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2025/1012), [CRC ICLR 2024](https://openreview.net/forum?id=33XGfHLtZg), [De Toni et al. 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.16829).

SCORE_POSITIONING: 6.5/10. Single most important fix: add the missing CRC-in-recommender/ranking neighbors and sharpen the claim around conditional served-slice risk, not “CRC applied to NDCG.”
